With the Meta XR Core SDK, each tracked hand has an `OVRHand` component that exposes pinch state per finger:

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class PinchDetector : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] OVRHand hand;                // drag in OVRHandPrefab's OVRHand
    [SerializeField, Range(0, 1)] float threshold = 0.8f;
    bool wasPinching;

    void Update()
    {
        if (!hand.IsTracked || hand.HandConfidence != OVRHand.TrackingConfidence.High) return;

        float strength = hand.GetFingerPinchStrength(OVRHand.HandFinger.Index);
        bool pinching = strength > threshold;

        if (pinching && !wasPinching) OnPinchStart();
        if (!pinching && wasPinching) OnPinchEnd();
        wasPinching = pinching;
    }

    void OnPinchStart() => Debug.Log("Pinch started");
    void OnPinchEnd() => Debug.Log("Pinch ended");
}
```

Tips:

- Use `GetFingerPinchStrength` with a threshold plus a little hysteresis (for example start at 0.8 and release at 0.6) rather than `GetFingerIsPinching`, to avoid flicker.
- Ignore frames with low confidence; tracking degrades when fingers are occluded.
- If you use the Interaction SDK, prefer its `HandGrabInteractor` and pose selectors over hand-rolled checks.
- With the vendor-neutral OpenXR route, use the XR Hands package (`XRHandSubsystem`) and measure the thumb-tip to index-tip distance instead (roughly under 2 cm counts as a pinch).

Use **Unity Inference Engine** (the successor to Sentis; package `com.unity.ai.inference`, or `com.unity.sentis` on older versions):

```csharp
using Unity.InferenceEngine;   // `using Unity.Sentis;` on Sentis 2.x
using UnityEngine;

public class OnnxRunner : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] ModelAsset modelAsset;   // drag your .onnx here
    Worker worker;

    void Start()
    {
        var model = ModelLoader.Load(modelAsset);
        worker = new Worker(model, BackendType.GPUCompute); // CPU for tiny models
    }

    public float[] Run(float[] input, int features)
    {
        using var x = new Tensor<float>(new TensorShape(1, features), input);
        worker.Schedule(x);
        using var y = (worker.PeekOutput() as Tensor<float>).ReadbackAndClone();
        return y.DownloadToArray();
    }

    void OnDestroy() => worker?.Dispose();
}
```

Notes:

- Export from PyTorch with `torch.onnx.export(..., opset_version=17)` and fixed input shapes where you can.
- On Quest, small MLPs and transformers often run faster on `BackendType.CPU` than on the GPU, because readback is expensive. Benchmark both.
- Spread heavy models over several frames (`ScheduleIterable`) to avoid frame drops.
- Dispose tensors and workers, or you'll leak native memory.

```html
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from "https://unpkg.com/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js";
import { VRButton } from "https://unpkg.com/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/webxr/VRButton.js";

const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
renderer.setSize(innerWidth, innerHeight);
renderer.xr.enabled = true;
document.body.append(renderer.domElement, VRButton.createButton(renderer));

const scene = new THREE.Scene();
const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(70, innerWidth / innerHeight, 0.01, 50);
scene.add(new THREE.HemisphereLight(0xffffff, 0x444466, 2));

const cube = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.3, 0.3, 0.3), new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x3ee6c1 }));
cube.position.set(0, 1.5, -1);
scene.add(cube);

renderer.setAnimationLoop((t) => {
  cube.rotation.y = t / 1000;
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
});
</script>
```

Key points:

- Use `renderer.setAnimationLoop`, not `requestAnimationFrame`. The headset drives the frame loop.
- WebXR needs **HTTPS** (or localhost).
- In VR the camera is placed by the headset, so put content at about 1.5 m height and 1 m in front.
- In React, `@react-three/xr` wraps this: `createXRStore()` plus `<XR store={store}>` and `store.enterVR()`.
- Test without a headset using the Immersive Web Emulator browser extension.

With the Meta XR SDK:

```csharp
// amplitude 0..1, frequency 0..1, on the right controller
OVRInput.SetControllerVibration(0.5f, 0.8f, OVRInput.Controller.RTouch);
// stop
OVRInput.SetControllerVibration(0, 0, OVRInput.Controller.RTouch);
```

Vibration stops automatically after about 2 seconds, so call it again for longer effects. For richer effects, design clips in **Meta Haptics Studio** and play them with the **Meta XR Haptics SDK** (`HapticClipPlayer`).

With the OpenXR / XR Interaction Toolkit:

```csharp
using UnityEngine.XR.Interaction.Toolkit.Inputs.Haptics;

[SerializeField] HapticImpulsePlayer haptics;   // on the controller GameObject
haptics.SendHapticImpulse(0.6f, 0.1f);          // amplitude, duration in seconds
```

```js
const res = await fetch("https://quddus.diaspafra.org/api/v1/chat/completions", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: {
    Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.QUD_API_KEY}`,
    "Content-Type": "application/json",
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    model: "qud-1",
    stream: true,
    messages: [{ role: "user", content: "Write a Unity script that logs headset position." }],
  }),
});

const reader = res.body.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let buffer = "";
for (;;) {
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  buffer += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });
  const lines = buffer.split("\n");
  buffer = lines.pop();
  for (const line of lines) {
    if (!line.startsWith("data: ") || line === "data: [DONE]") continue;
    const delta = JSON.parse(line.slice(6)).choices[0]?.delta?.content;
    if (delta) process.stdout.write(delta);
  }
}
```

Or with the official SDK: `new OpenAI({ baseURL: "https://quddus.diaspafra.org/api/v1", apiKey })`, then `client.chat.completions.create({ model: "qud-1", stream: true, messages })`.

Call it from your server, not the browser, so your key stays private.
